{"amendments":[{"body":"Fix closed-system mass creation in `system:daily_shrimp`. In `crates/tank_core/src/systems/shrimp.rs:354` and `crates/tank_core/src/systems/shrimp.rs:371`, a resolved clutch adds `25` juveniles per successful berried female without debiting any explicit nitrogen/carbon source, so the new ledger violates the acceptance criterion once reproduction is enabled. Repro: starting from the existing shrimp fixture in `crates/tank_core/tests/shrimp_population.rs:8`, a 60-day no-feed/no-water-change run with budget tracking ends with positive mass drift (`delta_n ~= 65.38 mg`, `delta_c ~= 408.62 mg`), and the first non-zero closed tick is `system:daily_shrimp` at about `+34.48 mg N / +215.52 mg C` per hatch. Model clutch/egg biomass as an explicit pool and include it in the canonical totals, or debit another explicit source on spawn/hatch, and add a regression in `crates/tank_core/tests/budget_tracking.rs:404` covering a reproducing shrimp tank with no feed/export.","rationale":"The headline acceptance criterion is that closed-system ticks stay near zero. That is currently false for a realistic in-repo shrimp scenario, so the budget ledger will report a real conservation failure in normal gameplay states."},{"body":"Add an actual end-of-tick conservation guard when budget tracking is enabled. `crates/tank_core/src/engine.rs:164` and `crates/tank_core/src/engine.rs:168` only record and store the tick, while `crates/tank_core/src/types/budget.rs:141` uses only a `debug_assert!` for per-stage bookkeeping consistency. In release builds the ledger is passive, so non-conserving paths like the shrimp hatch case ship silently. Wire a tick-level closed-system check after invariants (or at least a test-only assert hooked into the tracked tick path) so a mass leak fails immediately instead of depending on ad hoc test coverage.","rationale":"This is a significant robustness gap: the new infrastructure is supposed to verify conservation, but today it only records numbers and relies on tests to notice regressions."}],"summary":"Reviewed the committed budget-tracking delta end-to-end (`engine.rs`, `budget.rs`, `state.rs`, `water_change.rs`, `dissolved_oxygen.rs`, `shrimp.rs`, and the new tests) and ran `cargo test -p tank_core`, which passes. I found one concrete correctness bug in shrimp reproduction mass accounting and one related guardrail gap in the tracked end-of-tick path."}